From: BananaDS <bananads@localhost>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:48:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NDS: two code regions kept for the swap back, the ARM9's BIOS
 and the ARM7's shared WRAM among them (RegionTrio)

Patch 0071 (RegionPair) keeps the one code region a CPU's PC last left, so a round through two regions is all
swaps. Mario Kart DS's race start still set regions up ~640 times a frame (qemu, frames 1650-2150): the ARM9's IRQs
go main RAM -> its BIOS -> an ITCM handler -> the BIOS -> main RAM (ITCM 191, BIOS 147, main RAM 103 a frame), and
the ARM7's sound driver runs in shared WRAM and goes through its BIOS (shared WRAM 118, BIOS 77, WRAM 8): three
regions, and two the pair doesn't keep. Each setup is ClassifyAddress, GetMirrorLocation and SetupExecutableRegion,
~115 instructions.

With RegionTrio (NDS::SetRegionTrio, on by default; with RegionPair) two regions are kept, the last one left and
the one before, and on a DS the ARM9's BIOS (a 4 KB mirror at 0xFFFF0000 with memoryOffset 0, put there by
ClassifyAddress9 under the same ITCM and DTCM checks as main RAM) and the ARM7's shared WRAM can be kept too. The
shared WRAM's lookup, start and size are a function of SWRAM_ARM7's Mem and Mask (memoryOffset = Mem - the shared
WRAM's start, the mirror Mask + 1), so a kept one is swapped back only while Mem and Mask are those its lookup and
size were set up with, for an address ClassifyAddress7 puts in the shared WRAM. A swap gives exactly the lookup,
start and size SetupExecutableRegion would. ARMv5/ARMv4::Execute<JIT> (the slice after a CPU wakes up, where an IRQ's round often runs) swaps too. On a DSi, RegionPair's own way. Off: RegionPair's own way, and Execute<JIT> sets up as before.
